I haven't called for a vote yet ;)

The selma'o BIhI consists of three words: bi'i (between), bi'o (from ...=20
to ...), and mi'i (within ... of ..., in reverse order). They are=20
conjunctions, not prepositions like the English words. All three can be=20
optionally modified by the words "ga'o" and "ke'i", which indicate whether=
=20
the ends of the interval are or are not included in the interval.

Currently, if GAhO is used with BIhI, there must be two, one on each side.=
=20
This is okay when the intervals are one-dimensional, but a=20
two-dimensional "interval" of the mi'i sort is a disk centered on the=20
referent of the first sumti. It is possible, and common in complex analysis=
,=20
for the boundary of a disk to be all closed except for one point, and it ca=
n=20
even be riddled with holes while not being completely open.

